<h2>The Harness Is Not Enough — Why Software Factories Fail</h2>

<p>At <strong>AI Engineer World's Fair 2026</strong>, <strong>Dex Horthy</strong> of HumanLayer gave a talk titled &ldquo;Harness Engineering is not Enough: Why Software Factories Fail&rdquo; that passed 250,000 views within a day. The argument fits in one sentence &mdash; <strong>if AI coding is degrading your codebase, it is not because you are holding it wrong; it is because the model was never trained for the thing you want.</strong></p>

<p>The distinction matters. If the problem were skill, the fix would be better prompts, tighter loops, more review bots &mdash; more <strong>harness</strong>. But if the cause sits in training, then no amount of harness can produce <strong>something the model never learned in the first place.</strong></p>

<h3>First, the cracks are measured, not felt</h3>

<p>The talk opens with telemetry rather than anecdote. Faros AI's <em>Acceleration Whiplash</em> report (March 2026) analyzed two years of engineering telemetry from <strong>22,000 developers across more than 4,000 teams</strong>.</p>

<div class="tbl-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Change</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Bugs per developer</td><td><strong>+54%</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Incidents &divide; pull requests</td><td><strong>more than tripled</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>Median review time</td><td><strong>5&times;</strong></td></tr>
<tr><td>PRs merged with no review</td><td><strong>+31.3%</strong></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>Throughput genuinely rose. But <strong>bugs and incidents rose faster than throughput.</strong> As Horthy puts it, companies that have no business having outages because of coding agents are having outages because of coding agents.</p>

<h3>They actually ran the lights-out factory</h3>

<p>The <strong>lights-out software factory</strong>, a term coined by Dan North, is this idea: nobody enjoys code review, so remove it. Invest instead in the <em>surrounding</em> systems &mdash; testing, monitoring, rollout &mdash; and redefine the human job as &ldquo;how much work can I put in the agent's queue?&rdquo;</p>

<p>HumanLayer did exactly this from <strong>July to November 2025</strong>. Nobody read the code. The talk summarizes the outcome:</p>

<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">What happened after three months</span>
    No matter how carefully you prompt, research, and reproduce, you eventually hit <strong>a bug the agent cannot solve</strong>. Then you have to go dig through <strong>a codebase you stopped reading three months ago</strong> to find what broke. Meanwhile your site is down, your users are angry, and you are miserable reading all the slop you let slip into the system.
</div>

<p>Note the timescale. &ldquo;Brownfield&rdquo; usually conjures a ten-year-old Java system, but Horthy says that at today's shipping pace <strong>agents start to struggle after just three to six months</strong>. This is not only a legacy problem.</p>

<h3>The core — models are trained on &ldquo;did the test pass&rdquo;</h3>

<p>Here is the substance of the talk: why the harness cannot fix this, explained through <strong>how reinforcement learning scores a coding agent</strong>.</p>

