<p>Take <strong>SWE-bench Multilingual</strong>, one of the classics. Its tasks come from open-source repositories like Redis, jq and Django, run about <strong>15 minutes</strong> each, and carry a <strong>binary 0 or 1 reward</strong>. The scoring procedure works like this:</p>

<ol>
    <li>Check out the commit from <em>before</em> a human fixed the issue.</li>
    <li>Let the agent solve it and store its patch.</li>
    <li><strong>Undo every change the agent made to test files</strong> &mdash; because models will happily comment out a test to make things green.</li>
    <li>Apply the hidden golden test and run it. Reward 1 <strong>only if the old tests still pass and the new test passes too.</strong></li>
</ol>

<p>Nowhere in that procedure is there <strong>a place to deduct points for worse design.</strong> So the model wraps things in <code>try/catch</code> that need no <code>try/catch</code>, and casts types that should not be cast. Passing the test is all that counts. In the talk's words &mdash; <strong>the model is just trying to get the test to pass.</strong></p>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">Why maintainability is hard to score</span>
    Because <strong>the cost function of bad architecture is measured in months and years.</strong> Right after a coding episode there is no signal at all. You find out that somebody vibed this a little too hard months later. A reward that arrives that late cannot drive reinforcement learning. Verifying that code <em>runs</em> and verifying that it is <em>maintainable</em> differ by orders of magnitude.
</div>

<h3>So why not build a benchmark that scores quality?</h3>

<p>People are trying, and the talk acknowledges it. The most concrete effort is Cognition's <strong>FrontierCode</strong>. Its tasks were built with open-source maintainers, take <strong>40+ hours each</strong>, and are scored on far more than test passage &mdash; <strong>regression safety, cleanliness, scope, test correctness and maintainability</strong>. Its false-positive rate is <strong>81% lower</strong> than SWE-Bench Pro. Tasks are split into Extended (150), Main (100) and Diamond (the 50 hardest).</p>

<p>The result is telling. <strong>The best model scores roughly 13% on the hardest subset</strong> &mdash; nothing like the 50%+ regime common on SWE-bench-style evals, which suggests the impression that &ldquo;coding is solved&rdquo; may say more about <strong>how easy the benchmarks were</strong>.</p>

<p>Horthy still draws a line here. Having models judge code quality has a ceiling:</p>

<div class="note warn">
    <span class="nh">The sharpest line in the talk</span>
    <strong>&ldquo;If the model knew what good code looks like, it would probably write it in the first place.&rdquo;</strong><br>
    Review agents and more tokens <strong>can raise the floor</strong>, but they remain bounded by what we can teach during RL.
</div>

<h3>The conclusion — we still have to read the code, but less of it</h3>

<p>This is where the talk parts ways with ordinary pessimism. Horthy's prescription is not &ldquo;use less AI&rdquo; but <strong>&ldquo;spend 30 minutes before any code is written.&rdquo;</strong> Align up front and the resulting code becomes predictable &mdash; which is what makes reading all of it realistic again.</p>

