<p>The four steps, unpacked:</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>① Product review</strong> &mdash; what problem are we solving, what is the desired behavior, with mock-ups if useful.</li>
    <li><strong>② System architecture</strong> &mdash; component contracts, data models, constraints. Many teams already do this one.</li>
    <li><strong>③ Program design</strong> &mdash; the step Horthy calls <strong>badly under-emphasized</strong>. People assume that once the architecture is right the model can just cook, but in practice you have to go down to <strong>types, method signatures, program layout and call stacks</strong>. Dylan Mulroy at Cloudflare using call graphs in his planning is the same instinct.</li>
    <li><strong>④ Vertical slices</strong> &mdash; the order of implementation, coordination across repositories, and what gets checked at each phase.</li>
</ul>

<p>One important caveat: <strong>small things still go straight to the agent.</strong> This is a tool for hard problems in complex codebases, not a ritual to impose on every change.</p>

<h3>&ldquo;You don't have too many PRs. You have too many bad PRs.&rdquo;</h3>

<p>The talk also inverts the belief that review is the bottleneck. <strong>A good PR is a joy to review</strong> &mdash; you read through it thinking &ldquo;yep, this is what we discussed.&rdquo; A PR that needs even 20% rework (generous for AI-vibed code) is <strong>an emotional and intellectual burden on both reviewer and submitter.</strong></p>

<p>So up-front alignment does not buy &ldquo;slow but safe.&rdquo; Three things speed up at once:</p>

<div class="tbl-wrap">
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Why it gets faster</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Alignment</td><td>AI gathers all the information at once, so it is shorter</td></tr>
<tr><td>Coding</td><td>AI does it</td></tr>
<tr><td>Review</td><td>You aligned up front, so you are just reading</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</div>

<p>The net effect: <strong>you really do move faster, while still reading everything and still owning the code.</strong></p>

<h3>How to take the argument</h3>

<p>The talk narrows its own scope. Horthy quotes Addy Osmani to draw the boundary &mdash; <em>&ldquo;A developer vibe coding a side project a dozen people will ever run and a team keeping a 10-year-old enterprise system alive for another quarter share almost no constraints worth naming.&rdquo;</em> Most advice on the internet is <strong>one of these groups telling the other how to live their lives.</strong></p>

<p>Horthy is explicit about it: <strong>if you love vibe coding, please go on.</strong> What this talk targets is <strong>hard problems in complex codebases.</strong></p>

<p>It is also worth reading honestly. This rests on <strong>one company's four-month experiment and the models of one moment.</strong> Horthy leaves the door open &mdash; &ldquo;if you want to just keep yoloing prompts until you get to GPT-7, by all means.&rdquo; The very existence of FrontierCode, actually scoring maintainability, is a signal that the limit may not be permanent.</p>

<p>But on the question of <strong>what to do this quarter</strong>, the measured data (Faros), the failed experiment (HumanLayer) and the structural limit of the benchmarks (reward = tests pass) all point the same way. The tools covered in this wiki's <a href="#!hns-overview">harness engineering</a> series remain useful &mdash; but as the title says, <strong>they are not enough on their own.</strong></p>

